1706: transcript for the submitted records, and its negative run returns the reported
1707: finite error \path{neg1: command-run fields incomplete}.  By
1708: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, those are the only two output
1709: kinds of the verifier contract: acceptance of the six predicates or a bad
1710: subrecord for the first failed predicate.  By
1711: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, positive acceptance is equivalent
1712: to \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}\) for the same tuple
1713: \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\).  In the negative fixture, clauses A1--A4 and the
1714: non-discovery discipline are supplied for the route row, while the declared
1715: command-run level triggers clause A6 and the required five command-run fields are
1716: absent.  The first failed predicate therefore reads exactly \(\ell\) and \(B\),
1717: so deleting any unrelated prose, score, route history, or ambient implementation
1718: state leaves the same failure.  The script is thus an executable finite-record
1719: witness for the schema-relative theorem and for its negative certificate example,
1720: not evidence of an unrecorded semantic rerun or gate-implementation proof.\end{proof}
1721: 
1722: \begin{theorem}[Finite-coordinate determination and trust-boundary classifier]
1723: \label{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}
1724: Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$ and the verifier $V$.  Let $F_{\Gamma}$ be the
1725: finite schema projection read by $V$ from a certificate record, namely the
1726: claim-kind set, declared evidence level, evidence surface, promotion field,
1727: gate-pass rows, discovery-source rows, command-boundary tuple, and the assigned
1728: gate set determined by $\Gamma$.  If two ambient pipeline or artifact states
1729: induce the same values of all coordinates in $F_{\Gamma}$ for a claim $c$, then
1730: they induce the same record-acceptance decision and the same finite publication
1731: certificate for $c$.  Therefore the exactness of
1732: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} is exactness only relative to this
1733: finite schema projection.  No property depending on a coordinate outside
1734: $F_{\Gamma}$---for example the semantic truth of a referenced theorem, truthful
1735: production or implementation soundness of a gate row, a fresh Lean build result,
1736: a daemon trace, an artifact semantic fact, a theorem-environment completeness
1737: claim, or an external venue decision---is a consequence of the accepted
1738: certificate unless that property is first encoded as an additional finite
1739: certificate coordinate and the enlarged record is accepted.  In particular,
1740: record-gate soundness is a load-bearing hypothesis for
1741: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}; it is not supplied by schema
1742: exactness alone.
1743: \end{theorem}
1744: 
1745: \begin{proof}
1746: The verifier $V$ is defined as an ordered list of six checks, and each check reads
1747: only one of the coordinates listed in $F_{\Gamma}$ together with the fixed finite
1748: dispatch table $\Gamma$.  If two ambient states induce the same values on
1749: $F_{\Gamma}$, then the input tuple \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\), the assigned gate set,
1750: and every submitted pointer queried by $V$ are identical in the two states.  Each
1751: of the six checks therefore has the same truth value in the two states, so $V$
1752: either accepts both records or rejects both records at the same first failed
1753: finite field.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, an accepted
1754: decision is equivalent to the same six-clause record-acceptance semantics, hence
1755: the finite certificate determined by those clauses is also the same.  This proves
1756: the schema-relative and finite-record-relative factorisation.
1757: 
1758: Now let $Q$ be a property whose truth can vary while all coordinates in
1759: $F_{\Gamma}$ are held fixed.  Then there exist two ambient states with the same
1760: certificate tuple and the same verifier decision but different truth values for
1761: $Q$.  A conclusion whose truth changes under such an $F_{\Gamma}$-preserving
1762: substitution is not determined by the accepted certificate.  To make $Q$
1763: load-bearing, the operational interface must add a finite coordinate recording
1764: the required evidence: for a build or daemon trace this is a command-run tuple;
1765: for an artifact semantic fact this is an artifact pass record or fresh evaluator
1766: rerun log; for theorem-environment completeness this is an extractor command,
1767: source state, environment, exit code, and log path; and for a venue decision this
1768: is an external evidence surface and the corresponding route/submission record.
1769: Without that enlargement, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} supplies
1770: only the original finite-record conclusion.
1771: 
1772: Finally, the same argument isolates record-gate soundness.  The predicate
1773: $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ says that recorded ledger entries actually contain
1774: the assigned passing rows and submitted evidence pointers.  A package can agree
1775: on the schema shape of a candidate certificate while differing on whether such a
1776: record-gate-sound ledger has been supplied.  Thus schema exactness checks the
1777: finite acceptance relation after the row data are present; it does not by itself
1778: make those rows available, truthfully produced, or semantically adequate.
1779: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} therefore states record-gate
1780: soundness as an explicit hypothesis, and the trust boundary is exactly the finite
1781: projection $F_{\Gamma}$ plus that hypothesis.
1782: \end{proof}
1783: 
1784: \begin{theorem}[Record-interface rigidity and refresh fixed point]
1785: \label{thm:record-interface-rigidity}
1786: Fix a package $P$, submitted support surface $S$, finite ledger $L$, dispatch
1787: table $\Gamma$, and certificate tuple
1788: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ for each accepted load-bearing claim
1789: $c\in L$.  Let $\pi_{\Gamma,S}(P,L)$ be the finite projection consisting of the
1790: tuple fields $(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$, the gate-pass rows in $R$, the submitted
1791: support-surface membership relation for $S$, and the fixed dispatch table
1792: $\Gamma$.  Then the following are equivalent.
1793: \begin{enumerate}
1794: \item The ledger satisfies the external record-gate interface
1795: \(\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)\) and the verifier accepts every displayed
1796: certificate.
1797: \item The finite projection $\pi_{\Gamma,S}(P,L)$ is a fixed point of the six
1798: schema checks: re-reading the projected rows, evidence pointers, gate assignments,
1799: promotion field, discovery field, and command-boundary field reconstructs the
1800: same family of certificates and produces no new accepted coordinate.
1801: \end{enumerate}
1802: Moreover this fixed point is rigid.  Any proper stronger reading of an accepted
1803: claim---including gate-implementation soundness, fresh command rerun success,
1804: artifact-semantic validity, semantic theorem-inventory completeness, upload
1805: status, or venue acceptance---is accepted by the same interface if and only if it
1806: adds a nonempty finite extension coordinate to
1807: $(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ or to $R$ and the enlarged projection again satisfies the
1808: fixed-point condition.  In particular, after a submitted support file used by the
1809: projection is regenerated, the current-package finite-record claim is restored
1810: only by the finite refresh sequence that regenerates the dependent theorem
1811: environment inventory, certificate-verifier log, source-interface-verifier log,
1812: primary-claim verifier log, and final digest surface so that all paths and digest
1813: rows are again read from one common projection.  Without that common projection,
1814: the TeX theorem chain retains only its source-level statements and not a
1815: machine-readable current-package exactness claim.
1816: \end{theorem}
1817: 
1818: \begin{proof}
1819: Assume first that \(\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)\) holds and that every displayed
1820: certificate is accepted.  By
1821: Proposition~\ref{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness}, the
1822: interface supplies exactly the certificates whose evidence surfaces lie in $S$,
1823: whose assigned gate rows are present and passing, whose evidence pointers denote
1824: submitted elements at the recorded evidence level, and whose promotion,
1825: discovery, and command-boundary fields satisfy the verifier predicates.  Applying
1826: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} to each row identifies this with
1827: the six-clause relation $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}$.  Hence re-reading precisely the
1828: fields in $\pi_{\Gamma,S}(P,L)$ repeats the same six checks and returns the same
1829: certificate family; no seventh field or stronger coordinate is read by any check.
1830: This is the stated fixed point.
1831: 
1832: Conversely suppose the finite projection is such a fixed point.  The fixed-point
1833: condition states, field by field, that the kind and evidence-level fields pass
1834: the domain check, that every evidence pointer lies on $S$, that every assigned
1835: gate in $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$ has a passing row in $R$, that the promotion
1836: coordinate is present exactly when required, and that the discovery and
1837: command-boundary coordinates satisfy the presence conditions of $P_5$ and $P_6$.
1838: These are exactly the positive-output clauses of
1839: Definition~\ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface}.  Therefore
1840: \(\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)\) holds, and
1841: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} gives verifier acceptance for the
1842: same displayed certificates.
1843: 
1844: It remains to prove rigidity.  Let $c^{+}$ be a proper stronger reading of an
1845: accepted claim.  By Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}, being proper
1846: means that at least one of the finite coordinates $K$, $\ell$, $E$, $H$, $R$,
1847: $D$, or $B$ is strictly enlarged.  If the enlarged tuple and any added pass rows
1848: again satisfy the six schema checks, the preceding equivalence applied to the
1849: enlarged projection accepts $c^{+}$.  Conversely, if $c^{+}$ is accepted by the
1850: same finite interface, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}
1851: forces the acceptance decision to be determined by a finite coordinate read by
1852: the verifier.  Since $c^{+}$ is not record-equivalent to $c$, at least one such
1853: coordinate is new or strictly enlarged; otherwise the two readings would have the
1854: same projection and hence the same bounded certificate meaning.  Thus every
1855: proper stronger reading has positive finite extension budget and no stronger
1856: reading is obtained for free from the original fixed point.
1857: 
1858: The listed examples are exactly instances of this coordinate test.  Gate
1859: implementation soundness requires implementation source, specification,
1860: environment, command or implementation-gate records, and logs.  Fresh rerun
1861: success requires command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path.
1862: Artifact-semantic validity requires an artifact-semantic evidence surface or
1863: evaluator pass row.  Semantic theorem-inventory completeness requires an
1864: extractor command, source-to-inventory binding, produced inventory, and pass row.
1865: Upload and venue readings require external route, submission, or decision
1866: records.  None is among the old projection unless it is explicitly added.
1867: 
1868: Finally suppose a file on the submitted support surface is regenerated.  The
1869: membership and digest facts read by $P_2$, the pass-row facts read by $P_3$, and
1870: the command-boundary facts read by $P_6$ then refer to a changed byte coordinate.
1871: The fixed-point implication can be applied again only after the finite surfaces
1872: that mirror that coordinate have been recomputed from the same bytes: the theorem
1873: environment extractor supplies the theorem-inventory coordinate, the certificate
1874: verifier supplies the schema-acceptance coordinate, the source-interface verifier
1875: supplies the imported-source coordinate, the primary-claim verifier supplies the
1876: retained-claim coordinate, and the final digest surface supplies the byte identity
1877: shared by the submitted support relation.  If one of these surfaces is stale, two
1878: ambient states can agree on the printed TeX prose while disagreeing on a byte
1879: coordinate read by the projection, so the machine-readable current-package
1880: exactness conclusion is not determined.  What remains is only the TeX-level
1881: statement already proved independently of that regenerated support surface.
1882: \end{proof}
1883: 
1884: \begin{theorem}[Record-gate implementation overclaim obstruction]
1885: \label{thm:record-gate-implementation-overclaim-obstruction}
1886: The hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} and the fixed
1887: point of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-interface-rigidity} determine finite certificate
1888: construction under the external record-gate interface
1889: \(\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)\), but they do not determine correctness of any
1890: gate implementation that produced, stored, or displayed the pass rows.  More
1891: precisely, for any accepted claim row $c$, the following are equivalent.
1892: \begin{enumerate}
1893: \item The same finite-record calculus accepts an implementation-soundness reading
1894: for the gates assigned to $c$.
1895: \item The package support surface contains a nonempty implementation coordinate
1896: for those gates: gate source identity, source digest or commit, command line,
1897: runtime environment, input records, output records, exit code, log path, digest
1898: binding to the submitted support surface, and an assigned implementation-gate
1899: pass row.
1900: \end{enumerate}
1901: Without this coordinate, every implementation-soundness reading has a finite bad
1902: subrecord, while the bounded certificate-construction reading remains valid under
1903: record-gate soundness.
1904: \end{theorem}
1905: 
1906: \begin{proof}
1907: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} uses
1908: \(\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)\) as an external hypothesis: the required finite
1909: pass rows and evidence pointers are present on the submitted surface.  It then
1910: constructs the certificate tuple read by the schema verifier.  Proposition~\ref{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness}
1911: and Theorem~\ref{thm:record-interface-rigidity} show that the finite projection
1912: contains the certificate fields, the assigned pass rows, the submitted-surface
1913: membership relation, and the dispatch table, and that no implementation source,
1914: runtime, input/output trace, or semantic correctness predicate is read unless it
1915: is included as an additional coordinate.
1916: 
1917: Suppose first that an implementation-soundness reading is accepted.  If no
1918: implementation coordinate of the displayed form were present, two review states
1919: could agree on every field in the record-gate projection and differ only in the
1920: unread gate implementation: in one state the implementation is correct, and in
1921: another it is faulty or not submitted at all, while the displayed pass-row record
1922: is the same.  The verifier would give the same finite answer on both states.  By
1923: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, a conclusion that
1924: distinguishes them cannot be determined by the current projection.  Hence the
1925: accepted implementation-soundness reading must add a nonempty implementation
1926: coordinate, and omission of that coordinate is the finite bad subrecord named by
1927: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.
1928: 
1929: Conversely, if the displayed implementation coordinate and its assigned pass row
1930: are present, then the implementation reading is no longer a free strengthening.
1931: It is an ordinary extension coordinate in the preorder of
1932: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  The enlarged tuple is accepted
1933: exactly when the six schema checks and the additional assigned implementation
1934: gate row form the fixed point of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-interface-rigidity}.
1935: Thus the theorem proves a sharp dichotomy: the current package gives bounded
1936: finite certificate construction under record-gate soundness, and implementation
1937: soundness appears only after the stated finite support extension.
1938: \end{proof}
1939: 
1940: \begin{corollary}[Finite-record acceptance is not implementation soundness]
1941: \label{cor:finite-record-acceptance-not-implementation-soundness}
1942: The acceptance and fixed-point conclusions of
1943: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-interface-rigidity} and
1944: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-gate-implementation-overclaim-obstruction} are finite
1945: record conclusions only.  They do not imply Lean rebuild success, BEDC
1946: source-tree rebuild success, publication-daemon soundness, overlap-checker
1947: soundness, artifact evaluator soundness, venue upload, or venue acceptance.  Any
1948: one of these stronger readings is accepted by the same theorem chain if and only
1949: if the submitted support state adds the corresponding finite implementation or
1950: external-decision coordinate, including source state, command or implementation
1951: contract, environment, exit code, log path, digest binding, and assigned pass row
1952: where that coordinate is command-like.
1953: \end{corollary}
1954: 
1955: \begin{proof}
1956: The two cited theorems identify the accepted data as a finite projection: record
1957: fields, pass rows, support-surface membership, dispatch-table entries, and any
1958: explicitly added implementation coordinate.  The listed stronger readings depend
1959: on coordinates not present in that projection unless separately supplied.  Lean
1960: and BEDC rebuild success read formal source state, toolchain, command, exit code,
1961: and log fields.  Daemon, overlap-checker, and evaluator soundness read
1962: implementation contracts, inputs, outputs, runs, semantic pass rows, or evaluator
1963: evidence.  Venue upload and acceptance read external route, submission, receipt,
1964: and decision fields.  Two ambient states can agree on the accepted finite
1965: projection while differing in any one of these absent coordinates; therefore
1966: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} forbids deriving the
1967: stronger reading from record acceptance alone.
1968: 
1969: Conversely, once the relevant coordinate and assigned pass row are included, the
1970: reading is a proper extension in the preorder of
1971: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  The finite extension-budget
1972: normal form, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, and the fixed
1973: point of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-interface-rigidity} accept exactly that enlarged
1974: projection and nothing beyond it.  Thus finite-record acceptance is sharp: it is
1975: not a hidden proof of implementation, daemon, artifact, upload, or venue
1976: soundness.
1977: \end{proof}
1978: 
1979: \begin{proposition}[Machine-readable certificate records]
1980: \label{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}
1981: For the current presentation package, the finite record checker
1982: $V$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}, with the
1983: record-acceptance semantics of
1984: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, has an executable record-level
1985: instance over the review bundle, conditional on exact supplemental inclusion of
1986: \path{review_bundle/} in the completed submission with matching final digests, as stated in
1987: Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}.  The schema file
1988: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json}, the pass-record ledger
1989: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json}, the primary-interface map
1990: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json}, the primary-claim inventory
1991: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, the verifier script
1992: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}, and the recorded run
1993: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log} instantiate the verifier at
1994: the declared evidence levels for entries $a_1,\ldots,a_7$.  The negative fixture
1995: \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json} and recorded run
1996: \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example_run.md} instantiate the first
1997: failure mode for a forbidden command-run escalation.  Each pass record in the
1998: ledger has the five fields \emph{gate name}, \emph{input surface},
1999: \emph{decision}, \emph{evidence pointer}, and \emph{evidence level}.  The
2000: machine-readable submission-interface map is locked to rows \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}
2001: of Table~\ref{tab:submission-interface-map} by its row identifiers, certificates,
2002: surfaces, and evidence levels; the primary-claim inventory is locked to rows
2003: \path{pc1}--\path{pc12} by its row identifiers, source locations, row classes,
2004: and evidence levels.  These executable locks check the finite record interface;
2005: full semantic equality between prose paraphrases and mathematical meaning,
2006: soundness of the gate implementations, and path completeness beyond the declared
2007: surfaces remain outside the record-check claim and stay at the manual audit
2008: boundary stated in the row classes.
2009: \end{proposition}
2010: 
2011: \begin{proof}
2012: All objects in the statement are finite files in the submitted review bundle, so
2013: the claim is a finite record check.  The manifest
2014: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json} contains top-level fields named
2015: \path{certificate_schema}, \path{current_package_pass_records},
2016: \path{submission_interface_map}, \path{primary_claim_inventory},
2017: \path{verifier_script}, and \path{verifier_run_log}; these fields point to the verifier
2018: bundle files named in the statement.  Thus the executable verifier is not an
2019: implicit prose convention but a submitted artifact surface.
2020: 
2021: The schema file lists the seven claim kinds, the two evidence levels, the same
2022: gate dispatch $\Gamma$ used in the supplement, and the required pass-record
2023: fields $(g,I,d,e,\ell)$.  The pass-record ledger has exactly seven entries,
2024: $a_1,\ldots,a_7$.  For each entry it records a nonempty claim-kind set, the
2025: path-verified evidence level, a finite evidence surface, the route-boundary or
2026: human-promotion field, pass records, the discovery-interface field, and the
2027: evidence boundary.  Inspecting the seven entries gives the required gate coverage:
2028: $a_1$ has inventory and marker-existence records; $a_2$ has promotion, overlap,
2029: submission-state, and submission-pack records; $a_3$ has promotion, overlap, and
2030: submission-state records; $a_4$ has promotion, overlap, submission-state, and
2031: submission-pack records; $a_5$ has pinned-discovery and theorem-content-delta
2032: records; and $a_6$ has status and limitation-ledger records.  These are exactly
2033: the unions of the gate sets assigned by $\Gamma$ to the claim-kind sets displayed
2034: in Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}.  The only row with discovery kind
2035: is $a_5$, and it is the only row with a non-null discovery-interface field; every
2036: path-verified row carries a boundary saying that no command-run assertion is
2037: made.  The seventh row $a_7$ is the bibliography/venue-freshness traceability and
2038: submission-pack record; it is path-verified in the acceptance ledger, while the
2039: separate command-boundary inventory below records the live-check command log used
2040: for the bounded venue and bibliography freshness assertion.
2041: 
2042: The script \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py} implements precisely
2043: these finite checks: required manifest fields, entries $a_1$--$a_7$, kind-domain
2044: membership, evidence-level membership, assigned-gate coverage, the five
2045: pass-record fields, evidence-level agreement, discovery-field discipline, the
2046: path-verified boundary condition, exact row locks for \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}
2047: against the submission-interface JSON fields, and row-class locks for
2048: \path{pc1}--\path{pc12} against the primary-claim inventory locations, statuses,
2049: and evidence levels.  The recorded log
2050: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log} records a successful run of